=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
There is no fault in this fabric. All eight BGP sessions (leaf_0_0 ↔ spine_0_0, leaf_0_0 ↔ spine_0_1, leaf_0_1 ↔ spine_0_0, leaf_0_1 ↔ spine_0_1, spine_0_0 ↔ super_spine, spine_0_1 ↔ super_spine) are Established and have been continuously stable since lab start (~5 minutes ago). Every router shows "Connections established 1; dropped 0" with zero BGP NOTIFICATION messages in any log. Standard 180-second hold timers and 60-second keepalives are configured uniformly; there are no aggressive timers and no BFD. A sustained 10-packet ICMP test from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) delivers 10/10 with 0% loss at sub-2 ms RTT, confirming end-to-end data-plane reachability across the Clos fabric.

The one observational oddity — spine_router_0_1 showing 0 prefixes received from super_spine_router_0 — is entirely correct BGP behaviour: super_spine's best paths for 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 were both learned from spine_router_0_1, so eBGP split horizon prevents re-advertising them back. Both leaf routers have valid BGP routes to the opposite rack's prefix via the spine layer, as confirmed by their FIBs and the successful ping test.

The user's report of "sessions bouncing nonstop" is a mischaracterisation of what is actually a stable, correctly operating fabric. No corrective action is required. If the user is experiencing application-layer disruption, the cause lies outside the BGP/IP fabric (e.g., application timeouts, a higher-layer protocol, or a transient issue that pre-dated the current lab deployment).
=== END ===